 the city  where no one is likely to go the same  principle applies to more interesting  areas and iconic objects in our level as  well the more expensive or costly they  are we tend to put them in the middle  where people will get to see them  because who wants to work on a really  pretty object and then no one gets to  see it that wouldn't be fun so this is  cesar shanghai the left side is a spawn  based on the right side of the spawn  base and the tower in the middle is the  middle base and as you can see we we  took that principle to the extreme here  where the middle base is the giant  LEvolution destructible building that  falls down but there's also a higher  density of micro destruction props there  and macro destruction cascades out to  the other bases as well that way players  feel like they're getting some kind of  cool interact ability with the  environment that it is physical that it  does respond to what you're doing I want  to talk quickly about l'évolution  because that was a new feature that we  introduced in battlefield and really  it's just a way for the level to respond  to the player to evolve dynamically as  you're playing the changes are either  passive or active storytelling and  cosmetic or tactical with destruction or  level changing features they're not  always destruction it could be like  you'll see there the the raising of the  shutters when we were first testing a  lot of our l'évolution features however  we noticed that not a lot of people  realized they were there and when they  were triggered or something did happen  not a lot of people knew that it  happened so we decided after doing some  testing to approach it by informing the  player making them aware of what exists  by using mechanics and techniques that  are familiar to gamers like I said  flashing lights different materials the  red barrel that explodes we use this  sort of interaction language to help  inform the player of what's available  and what has happened we also needed to  reset crank it to 11 but we need to make  sure the player feels the event and  notices when something has changed so  that they're not confused when they come  back and suddenly the area is very  different so we have audio we have the  effects when you're closer to an event  but you're not really looking at it  sometimes you'll have a little screen  effect the most notable of this is  probably after the skyscraper comes down  on that level siege of Shanghai there's  a shader that gets placed on all the  objects it passes around there's effects  that go through the street and then a  dust shader that settles on all of the  objects so that even if you spawn into  the level after this has happened you  know the skyscraper is down so that way  you're not grabbing a sniper role you're  grabbing a tank to go annihilate people  at Point C we also wanted to add a bang  and maximize the potential and emotional  impact of all this which is why we like  added a big spectacle spectacle to  everything lots of effects over the top  so the goal with LEvolution was to add  new elements without removing focus from  our core battlefield experience and they  made a lot of changes throughout the way  we realized very early on that sometimes  the easiest levolution features to  implement like bollards raising up to  stop traffic often had the biggest  effect on gameplay so we asked the  entire studio to come up with ideas for  what levolution features they would like  to see what kind of interactable stuff  in the level they would like to see and  that generated a ton of ideas that we  sifted through and it helped create the  most effective mechanics to put effort  in time into our LEvolution pod is a  good example  of cross-disciplinary and work in the  industry and we're very excited that  they did well in that it demanded a lot  of constant testing but also a really  great technical understanding a  willingness to take on challenges and it  also needed a sense of imagination which  is probably one of the things that most  of us love about working in the games  industry being able to use the process  of rapid iteration the LEvolution team  was able to prototype rule out redo  refine and polish a lot of concepts from  ideation to implementation I'm just  going to show a quick video where our  producer is talking about some  l'évolution features it can be anything  like writing all new paths to get the  upper hand in the fight race bollards to  stop week fortify your position by  activating the strategy or how about  wrapping your enemy with one of your  hands names that one's my favorite  metal detectors will give me position  away  and car alarms can really screw up your  life  disorient your enemies by using the  environment to gain a tactical advantage  then there's the ambient changes that  will drastically alter the look at the  shader a detacher across the Bethel  finally you can trigger massive game  changing event you can flood an entire  city changing the title  LEvolution is a game-changer it's a big  focus for us as we take the dynamics of  battlefield it's a whole new level  pretty fun I like it now I go back and  play other FPS and I'm like why can't I  interact with everything I also wanted  to talk about our water and weather sub  system as you guys saw in the frostbite  video we have a new server synced water  simulation system that allows all of our  players to see the same waves at the  same time we ended up using an approach  that we call our visual target for a lot  of this because we have so much content  over so many maps we wanted to create a  base set of assets that could be used  everywhere that would react to whatever  environment they were put in so we had a  woodland visual target with trees that  were properly skinned and looked good  with foliage with terrain shaders with  water shaders that could be used on all  of those environments throughout the  game the same went for our ocean and  beach themed our islands and our  interior in our snowy sort of  environment so we had these visual  targets and then world builders or  designers could go in grab those shaders  grab those assets and they would  automatically work together in a scene  and then when you added wind or you  wanted to change the weather of the  level they would react and they would  look good under those conditions like i  said this demanded that a lot of our  content was very flexible and that was  probably one of the harder things to do  was go back and make shaders that would  look good when the water was completely  Placid but then would froth up and get a  little darker and look still solid like  good water when it became a lot more  stormy I wanted to show you guys our  visual target for the woodland  environment just so you get an idea and  this was really early on in production  so some of the assets got updated but  you'll still see kind of how we created  that  so as you can see we have around maybe  four or five different terrain shaders  here different scattered meshes on the  ground such as leaves or twigs grass on  the other side we have bushes we have  small sapling trees we have larger trees  and plenty of lens flares  we have to make sure that a lot of our  assets like rocks that were being used  for level design quite frequently we're  very solid so that from early on we're  getting good content into our levels  that we didn't have to go back in and  replace it with new content later on  also is really relaxing after being in a  place with a bunch of gunfire to just  drop into one of our visual targets have  that nice calm music so I wanted to say  a big thank you to all of you all of you  at GDC all of you who play our game  everybody that supports us in the  community big thank you from the  multiplayer team and also from the rest  of the battlefield 4 development team I  also want to extend a huge thank you to  the GDC board for organizing this event  and the technique technicians who keep  everything running smoothly and the  conference associate associates we  couldn't do any of this without you guys  so please warm round of applause
 

